Hi Alyssa — looping in a couple of practical recommendations and a minimal Node.js example you can drop into your service to handle SDK setup, retries, timeouts, and rate limit backoff. I also attached redwood-sdk-quickstart.js with the same sample for quick copy/paste.

Key recommendations (short):
- SDK: use Redwood's official SDK with connection pooling enabled for high QPS; prefer the streaming client for long-session chat to reduce memory and allow early responses.
- Endpoint mapping: for generation -> /v1/generate (default), for chat stateful sessions -> /v1/chat/sessions (supports KV cache). During migration, run 10-20% traffic to chat endpoint for canary.
- Timeouts: client-side socket timeout = 10s; request-level deadline = 6s for interactive flows. Set a longer server-side token generation deadline only for batch jobs.
- Retries: retry on 502/503/504 and connection resets. Use exponential backoff with jitter: base 200ms, factor 2, max 8s, max attempts 5. For 429 (rate limit) return Retry-After if present — respect it, then apply a randomized backoff 1.5-3x the Retry-After when traffic is bursty.
- Rate limits: prefer token-aware rate limiting (tokens/sec) over requests/sec if you vary payload size. Implement a leaky-bucket locally to smooth bursts.

Minimal Node.js sample (drop-in):

const Redwood = require('redwood-sdk');
const sdk = new Redwood.Client({
  apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY,
  baseUrl: process.env.REDWOOD_ENDPOINT || 'https://api.redwood.example/v1',
  poolSize: 8, // connection pool for high QPS
  timeoutMs: 6000 // per-request deadline (ms)
});

// retry wrapper util
async function callWithRetries(fn) {
  let attempt = 0;
  const maxAttempts = 5;
  let backoff = 200;
  while (true) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      attempt++;
      const status = err && err.statusCode;
      if (attempt >= maxAttempts) throw err;
      if (status === 401) throw err; // auth issues — don't retry
      if (status === 429) {
        const ra = parseInt(err.headers && err.headers['retry-after']) || 1;
        const jitter = Math.random() * ra;
        await sleep((ra * 1000 + jitter * 1000) * (1.5 + Math.random()));
      } else if ([502,503,504].includes(status) || !status) {
        await sleep(backoff + Math.random() * 100);
        backoff = Math.min(backoff * 2, 8000);
      } else {
        throw err;
      }
    }
  }
}

// example usage
async function generate(prompt) {
  return callWithRetries(() => sdk.generate({model: 'redwood-longchat-1', prompt, maxTokens: 256}));
}

function sleep(ms) { return new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); }


Migration notes: when you flip traffic to the chat sessions endpoint, make sure to pin model versions and enable KV prefix caching for user sessions to avoid cold cache slowdowns. For p95 latency targets, run a short load test with 20-30s session lengths and look for tail latency increases when the KV warms.

If you want, we can add a simple middleware for token accounting so Orbit can report per-request token cost for billing.

